  <entry>
    <id>http://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22030v2</id>
    <title>From Outliers to Topics in Language Models: Anticipating Trends in News Corpora</title>
    <updated>2026-09-19T09:19:53Z</updated>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22030v2" rel="alternate" type="text/html"/>
    <link href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22030v2" rel="related" type="application/pdf" title="pdf"/>
    <summary>This paper examines how outliers, often dismissed as noise in topic modeling, can act as weak signals of emerging topics in dynamic news corpora. Using vector embeddings from state-of-the-art language models and a cumulative clustering approach, we track their evolution over time in French and English news datasets focused on corporate social responsibility and climate change. The results reveal a consistent pattern: outliers tend to evolve into coherent topics over time across both models and languages.</summary>
    <category term="cs.CL" scheme="http://arxiv.org/schemas/atom"/>
    <published>2025-09-26T08:07:02Z</published>
    <arxiv:comment>presented at ICNLSP 2025; to appear in the ACL Anthology; received the Best Full Paper Award</arxiv:comment>
    <arxiv:primary_category term="cs.CL"/>
    <author>
